{"authored" [{"question" "q-160888" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the surface integral with upward orientation\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨z+y,\\,2y+z,\\,xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=x-y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$" "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-160888/review.json" "file_sha256" "05eae9be2f81f532abaa95cc617abaf602f50d8c1cdee8e06749a06bb8264c84" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-160888/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "b419ed3ac1560d9be7ec4b6500a3e25a0d2862b85b5666448ff957a9180b7d3b" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-160888/verification.json" "basis" 2391 "rationale" "The graph vector area (-1,1,1) is upward; substitution gives F=(x,x+y,xy), dot=y+xy, integral3. Reversing orientation gives−3. The original prompt omits orientation, and the authentic revealed solution computes exactly the upward formula and result3. Add only explicit upward wording and a short note, retaining all fields, five choices, correct-answer3, original worked solution, visual/source artifacts and historical question versions."} {"question" "q-160888" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-y)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-y)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨z+y,\\,2y+z,\\,xy⟩{|}_{z=x-y} \\\\  & =⟨(x-y)+y,\\,2y+(x-y),\\,xy⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{{x}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{x+y}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{xy}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z+y,\\,2y+z,\\,xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(x)\\cdot 1-(x+y)\\cdot (-1)+xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-x+x+y+xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y+xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(y+xy)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2+2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[2x+{x}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(2(1)+{1}^{2})-0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOrientation note: the vector area element $(-g_x,-g_y,1)\\,dx\\,dy=(-1,1,1)\\,dx\\,dy$ used above points upward. With this orientation the flux is $3$; reversing the orientation gives $-3$. The original prompt omitted the orientation, which is now explicit in the study question." "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-160888/review.json" "file_sha256" "05eae9be2f81f532abaa95cc617abaf602f50d8c1cdee8e06749a06bb8264c84" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-160888/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "b419ed3ac1560d9be7ec4b6500a3e25a0d2862b85b5666448ff957a9180b7d3b" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-160888/verification.json" "basis" 2391 "rationale" "The graph vector area (-1,1,1) is upward; substitution gives F=(x,x+y,xy), dot=y+xy, integral3. Reversing orientation gives−3. The original prompt omits orientation, and the authentic revealed solution computes exactly the upward formula and result3. Add only explicit upward wording and a short note, retaining all fields, five choices, correct-answer3, original worked solution, visual/source artifacts and historical question versions."} {"question" "q-160888" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-160888/review.json" "file_sha256" "05eae9be2f81f532abaa95cc617abaf602f50d8c1cdee8e06749a06bb8264c84" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-160888/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "b419ed3ac1560d9be7ec4b6500a3e25a0d2862b85b5666448ff957a9180b7d3b" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-160888/verification.json" "basis" 2391 "rationale" "The graph vector area (-1,1,1) is upward; substitution gives F=(x,x+y,xy), dot=y+xy, integral3. Reversing orientation gives−3. The original prompt omits orientation, and the authentic revealed solution computes exactly the upward formula and result3. Add only explicit upward wording and a short note, retaining all fields, five choices, correct-answer3, original worked solution, visual/source artifacts and historical question versions."} {"question" "q-160888" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨z+y,\\,2y+z,\\,xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=x-y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069555/state.json" "file_sha256" "9688e39da05c2ddc3a1115d98aa05fedaa658a524f97a566033103326a47863d" "basis" 2390 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069555/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-160888" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-y)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-y)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨z+y,\\,2y+z,\\,xy⟩{|}_{z=x-y} \\\\  & =⟨(x-y)+y,\\,2y+(x-y),\\,xy⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{{x}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{x+y}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{xy}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z+y,\\,2y+z,\\,xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(x)\\cdot 1-(x+y)\\cdot (-1)+xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-x+x+y+xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y+xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(y+xy)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2+2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[2x+{x}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(2(1)+{1}^{2})-0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069555/state.json" "file_sha256" "9688e39da05c2ddc3a1115d98aa05fedaa658a524f97a566033103326a47863d" "basis" 2390 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069555/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-160888" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069555/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "81ab6a811ad468a318f3ce1cabbf79fac6470bb34f6f35f7d0ae85c8fb40b982" "basis" 2390 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_